WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.628
FedRAMP High is a baseline for cloud services supporting high-impact federal use cases where security failures could have severe or catastrophic adverse effects.

2
00:00:10.628 --> 00:00:22.175
It connects impact categorization to stronger security expectations, engineering implementation, assessment evidence, authorization support, and continuous monitoring.

3
00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:26.456
High is demanding because the mission and data risk are demanding.

4
00:00:26.456 --> 00:00:33.032
The most important ideas are impact, scope, responsibility, evidence, resilience, and operations.

5
00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:35.709
Impact explains why High is appropriate.

6
00:00:35.709 --> 00:00:39.838
Scope defines the cloud service offering and its dependencies.

7
00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:46.261
Responsibility clarifies what the provider, customer, platform, and inherited services handle.

8
00:00:46.261 --> 00:00:49.166
Evidence supports assessment conclusions.

9
00:00:49.166 --> 00:00:55.360
Resilience and operations show whether the service can sustain its security posture over time.

10
00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.024
Success depends on disciplined engineering and honest documentation.

11
00:01:00.024 --> 00:01:13.482
Strong identity controls, privileged access management, segmentation, logging, monitoring, vulnerability management, change control, recovery planning, and secure development practices all matter.

12
00:01:13.482 --> 00:01:16.234
Documentation must match the live system.

13
00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:18.758
Inherited controls must be traceable.

14
00:01:18.758 --> 00:01:21.510
Customer responsibilities must be clear.

15
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:25.181
Unsupported claims are not enough for high-impact risk.

16
00:01:25.181 --> 00:01:29.233
The goal is not simply to pass a review or claim a stronger label.

17
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:36.039
The goal is to help federal stakeholders make informed risk decisions about high-impact cloud services.

18
00:01:36.039 --> 00:01:50.261
A strong High program gives those stakeholders a credible picture of the system boundary, control implementation, evidence, known risks, remediation work, resilience posture, and continuing monitoring discipline.
